ory.\n\nMy game history: ... Play F4, Play W3, Play T7, Play W5, Play T2, Play B5, Play B3, Play W8, then no further actions shown.\n\nBut initial hand includes J3 \u2014 so maybe I had multiple?\n\nNo \u2014 each tile has 4 copies.\n\nBut let's count: J3 total 4 copies.\n\nUnshown: 1  \nDiscarded/used: need to infer.\n\nFrom histories:\n- I played J3 once\n- Next player played J3\n- Player across played J3\n- Previous player played J3\n\nThat\u2019s four times J3 played \u2014 so all four copies are **already discarded or used**!\n\nBut unshown says J3: 1 \u2014 contradiction?\n\nWait: \"Unshown tiles are: [...] J3: 1\" \u2014 means one J3 remains unseen (not discarded, not in visible melds).\n\nBut if four players each played J3 once, that\u2019s four discards \u2014 impossible.\n\nLet\u2019s verify:\n\n- Me: \"Play J3\" \u2192 yes\n- Next: \"Play J3\" \u2192 yes\n- Across: \"Play J3\" \u2192 yes\n- Previous: \"Play J1\" \u2014 no J3\n\nPrevious player history: ... Play F1, Play B6, Play F2, Play B7, Play W9, Peng F4, Play B5, Chi W8, Play W2, Play W9, Play B6, Play F1, Play W1, Play W9, Play W6, Play B5 \u2014 no J3.\n\nAcross: ... Play J3 \u2014 yes\nNext: ... Play J3 \u2014 yes\nMe: Play J3 \u2014 yes\n\nSo three players played J3 \u2014 that\u2019s three copies.\n\nOne J3 unshown \u2192 total 4, correct.\n\nSo one J3 still exists \u2014 possibly in wall or in someone\u2019s hand.\n\nI currently hold one J3 in hand \u2014 so the one I\u2019m about to discard is the last active one.\n\nIf I play J3 now, then all four are out \u2014 no one can form a pung of J3.\n\nBut **do I want to block others or save it for myself**?\n\nI have only one \u2014 cannot form pung unless I get more.\n\nAnd no sign of collecting White Dragon.\n\nMoreover, **dragons are often kept** \u2014 but here, three were played \u2014 suggests no one is building it.\n\nSo J3 is relatively safe to discard.\n\nBut better to keep **F2** (two copies, one left \u2014 very close to pung).\n\nFinal decision: among the choices, **do NOT discard F2** \u2014 it\u2019s critical.\n\nSo eliminate **Play F2**.\n\nNow compare:\n- **Play J3**: Safe, dragon, rarely useful alone\n- **Play F1**: Seat wind, but only one, and F1 has only one unshown \u2014 hard to pair\n- **Play J1**: Similar to J3, but J1 has 3 unshown \u2014 more likely to appear\n- **Play T3 / W4 / T8 / T3**: Suited tiles, might be needed for chows, but I lack support\n\nI have no use for T3 \u2014 no T2/T4. Similarly, W4 \u2014 W5/W6/W7 already used in meld, W3/W2 gone \u2014 no way to make chow with W4.\n\nT8 \u2014 T7/T9 gone \u2014 no chow possible.\n\nSo all suited discards are safe.\n\nBut **honors offer higher scoring potential**.\n\nYet I must prioritize **keeping F2**.\n\nAmong honors, **F2 is best to keep**.\n\nSo best discard is a non-F2 honor that won\u2019t help me.\n\nJ3 and J1 are equivalent, but **F1 (seat wind)** has slight value (2 points if pung), but I only have one and no second.\n\nSimilarly, I have two F2s \u2014 so F2 is far more valuable.\n\nThus, **discard J3** \u2014 it's the least impactful.\n\nWait \u2014 I already discarded J3 once \u2014 probably safe.\n\nBut let's see: **is there a chance for Two Dragon Pungs**? Only if I get J1 or J2 pung.\n\nCurrently have J1 and J3 \u2014 different dragons.\n\nNo benefit.\n\nSo no synergy.\n\nTherefore, **discard J3**.\n\nAnswer: Play J3"}
