y gone), so forming 1-2-3 or 2-3-4 in T is risky for others. T3 may be moderately safe.\n- **Play T8**: T8 has three copies unshown. T7 and T9 are both highly used (T9 gone), so 6-7-8 or 7-8-9 in T is hard to complete. Likely safe.\n- **Play F2**: I hold a pair of F2 (South Wind), which matches the prevalent wind (South). Discarding one from a pair risks giving opponents access to a meld, especially since F2 still has 2 unshown tiles. But if someone is waiting on F2 for a pung, they might win on it. High risk.\n- **Play J2**: Green Dragon. Two unshown. No signs of active dragon focus. J2 is not involved in any knitted patterns prominently. Moderate safety.\n- **Play T7**: Only one T7 remains unshown. Given T7 was played by me and others, and T6/T8 exist, it might complete a chow like 5-6-7 or 6-7-8. But T6 is low supply (2 left), T5 is nearly gone. Risk exists, but low probability due to scarcity.\n\nReason: Safety is paramount. F2 is dangerous to discard because it\u2019s a prevalent wind tile and I hold a pair\u2014discarding it could feed a winning hand. Similarly, J2 is a dragon, less risky than wind, but still valuable. T8 and T7 are in a suit where sequences are disrupted (T5, T6 scarce), making them safer. B1 and B3 are very safe due to B2 being extinct\u2014no one can form B1-B2-B3. Among these, **B1** stands out as safest: no sequence possible involving it in Bamboos, and it hasn\u2019t been heavily signaled. Also, keeping B3 slightly better than B1 for potential 1-3 wait? But no second B1 to pair, and no B2 anyway. So both B1 and B3 are deadwood.\n\nHowever, **B1** is the least threatening discard. It avoids breaking a potential pair (I only have one B1), doesn't expose a wind/dragon, and cannot contribute to any chow due to missing B2. Hence, B1 is the optimal safe discard.\n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
